Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 22:30:04 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: OLDIE:  BioHarvest, Inc.

This satire, referred to in Ray Cromwell's recent post, was posted during
the fruitful and combative discussion we had during the Hurricane Andrew
disaster last September.

Alexander Chislenko asked about diverting the hurricane (this even as it
was building, before any of us knew the scope of the destruction to come).
Not possible to divert a hurricane with foreseeable technology, but it
raised a good "test case." I think it was Robin Hanson who suggested a
"bidding system" (what else?) in which Communities A and B, for simplicity,
would make bids, with the losing bidder getting the money _and_ the
hurricane. The idea was to get communities to more accurately estimate the
real costs of having a hurricane hit them.

(Lots of interesting issues for PPL/private law/customary law/polycentric
law advocates to debate.) 

I raised the issue of the "lone objector"--suppose the hurricane is known
to be heading towards Community A (Homestead, Florida) and I live
peacefully in Community B (Dirtpoor, Florida). My neighbors see an
opportunity to, say, get their tarpaper shacks replaced by the "hurricane
fee" so they effectively "invite" the hurricane onto Dirtpoor. But suppose
even one person, me, objected? Isn't this aggression? Is this right? Is
this what PPL/customary law means?

(I don't think it does, but there are some real issues, discussed by David
Friedman and Bruce Benson in their books, yet to be resolved. Perry, Ray,
and Shawn (no longer reading the list, or at least no longer posting)
ardently defended the PPL approach, pointing out that statist systems are
no better, that the situation is unlikely to arise as we projected it, etc.
I don't want to start a mini-flame war here, now, by attempting to recap
their views.)

To illuminate in my own special way the issues, I wrote this satire. Take
it in the spirit in which it was intended. (Believe me, I support PPL. It's
essentially how we all interact with the world in nearly all
spheres--family, friends, this list, and on and on. Statist, or
centralized, law is the real anomaly.)

Ironically, the subject has popped up again, with the threads about PPL
compensation, selling body parts to pay debts, and cannibalism.


