From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Reincarnation vs. Migration to Silicon

(I've taken the liberty of simplifying the header. It used to be
"PHIL/DANCE: Reincarnation vs. Migration to Silicon" and I have removed the
"PHIL/DANCE:" prefix, as I didn't have a clue what "DANCE" had to do with
the thread, and "PHIL" is too general.)

Lefty (lefty@apple.com) asks a very good question:

>>Carol Moore writes:
>>> It suddenly occured to me -- why is the idea of
>>> humans storing or transfering their consciousness to
>>> silicon chips taken seriously in some circles,
>>> while the idea of transfering consciousness to another
>>> physical being is not?
>>
>>Because the former is falsifiable while the latter is not.
>
>Just out of pure, perverse curiousity, how is transferring consciousness to
>silicon more falisfiable than transferring consciousness to another
>physical being?

Speaking as a profound skeptic that uploading will happen anytime soon
(soon, as in the next 50 years), let me answer your question. (Some are
tired of hearing about reincarnation, I know. So am I. But until we have a
FAQ or an archive site of freely-postable articles....)

The objections to reincarnation:

First, "falsifiable" is not the first word I would use. We can't really
"falsify" the basic idea of reincarnation, though we can do tests to see
how likely it is someone's claim of having been a 16th century Tibetan
monk's love child--do they really know the language? Do they have specific
information that they couldn't have reasonably gotten otherwise? (It often
turns out in these cases, as Perry and others have noted, that the subject
read an obscure book on Tibetan monks, or maybe saw Eddie Murphy's "The
Golden Child" and simply unconsciously or consciously embroidered the
tale.). 

So in this sense, the claims of reincarnation are testable. Someone who
visits a foreign country can be grilled for information they could only
have gotten (probably) by actually visiting the country. For some
reason--draw your own conclusions--reincarnation claims never have this
exhaustive detail. Even allowing for "bandwidth loss" (degradation of
memories), one would expect these claimants to have knowledge that is
unknown to anyone else--and not verified until much later. (For example, I
would regard it as fairly compelling evidence if someone first described in
detail the house they lived in in a past life, the eating utensils, the
types of furniture and other hardware, etc., and then *independently and
separately* (to prevent collusion of the sort James Randi discusses) the
house was uncovered in an archaeological dig and the contents matched. That
would be convincing.)

Second, there is no plausible mechanism by which a "soul" can exist outside
of its meat computer. Put the brain into a coma and the "mind" (a
sophisticated "process" in computer terms) goes dormant. Unplug the brain
from life support and the mind simply _is not_. Dean Tribble wrote a nice
essay on this, last summer, entitled something like "Where does the soul go
when the body dies?" His angle was "Where does the process go when the
computer is turned off." 

There are related issues about how the "soul" would flow from one body to
another (would it go through a Faraday cage shield? Where does it sit while
the centuries are going by?). 

So what are the arguments in favor of "uploading"?

First, the Moravec argument (not clear who first used it) about replacing
one neuron with a silicon version, checking that the patient is still OK
and still maintains his identity, then replacing a second neuron, and so
on, until *all* of the neurons are replaced. This is one version of
uploading.

Although an incredible engineering challenge, the underlying concepts are
quite clear. No new undiscovered principles are needed, no hand-waving.

Second, the "silicon neuron" can just about as easily be a process running
on a computer, of whatever technology. This leads the most grandiose--but
still not violating any laws--version of uploading: minds as processes in
computers. Processes that can be halted, duplicated, stored on magnetic or
optical media, and perhaps even reversed (some unanswered issues, there!).

(There are still philosophical issues. These get debated regularly on this
list.)

In conclusion, reincarnation and uploading are only superficially similar.
There is no strong evidence for reincarnation and fairly compelling reasons
to doubt it is even remotely plausible. Uploading is a prodigious
engineering challenge, but uses existing principles and so should someday
be possible.

By the way, uploading is an important tenet of our cluster of Extropian
ideas. To ban discussion of uploading unless we give equal time to
reincarnation seems folly.

Hope this answers the question.

-Tim May

