There are apparently rules against perpetuities (except in Luxembourg?),
but I wonder if I could insure my reanimation along these lines:

(a) life insurance policy, payable to reanimation fund upon legal
death & suspension.  (This is in addition to life insurance policy
paid for suspension & storage).
(b) reanimation fund signs contract agreeing to pay for reanimation
and reimburse the balance, minus fee, to me upon reanimation. Since
there is no legal "me", "I" am defined by a key derived from my
DNA signature and kept in parts by the storage company, my most trustworthy 
descendants, trustworthy notaries public, etc. and reassembled 
by N-out-of-M voting when N of these agents reach a consensus about 
reanimating me.  N is adjusted if keys are lost, and keys are 
transfered upon holder's death under strict conditions (eg to closely 
related descendants, people who score very close to me on 
David Friedman's multi-dimensional memetic similarity test, etc.) Key
holders are paid a fee based on the success of the reanimation.  The degree 
of success is predefined in the contract by a set of psychological 
personality and memory tests taken before suspension and after 
reanimation.  Or, since such tests might be expected to greatly 
improve in quality during the suspension period, perhaps it's better 
to leave the definition of "success" up to a cryonics standards 
organization that does not itself hold keys.

Since the reanimation fund is invested in a well-diversified basket 
of mutual funds, bonds, gold, etc. it should be able to keep pace
with economic growth, even in an era of rapid growth, so the
fee should still mean something to these agents, and I should
have a reasonable amount with which to start my new life, assuming
I had enough to put into the fund to begin with!

All this is a crude attempt to resolve an important problem: how
can I set it up so that I am not brought out too early (crude tech &
high risk) or too late (more risk of organizational failure of
the storage firm & miss out on years of life)?  How can I ensure
my reanimation is carried out in my best interest when as a
suspendee I'm unable to influence events?

An interesting side note -- once I enter suspension I lose my
statist affiliation. I am now only recognized by the storage
company PPL; I've died & gone to voluntarist heaven. :-)  Of
course one can "die" in less painful ways, eg by faking suicide
off a bridge.  The bodies are often never found and the person
can set up somewhere else with fake ID.  Alas, the "somewhere
else" is nowadays likely to be controlled by a state...but
this may provide an untraceable form of emigration to PPL-freindly
territories, so that the PPL can avoid the extradition rap.

